The high-resolution studies of moisture-induced $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ degradation enabled by cryo-EM suggest a nanoscale degradation pathway (Figure 4H). Initially, an amorphous layer likely consisting of a hydrated compound coats the ${\mathsf{M A P b}}{\mathsf{b}}_{3}$ surface. This thin layer then facilitates decomposition of the $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ into gaseous components (e.g., HI and ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2})$ and nanograins of $\mathsf{P b l}_{2},$ further roughening the surface of the remaining $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ material. Interestingly, this degradation proceeds under much milder conditions (no UV irradiation, room temperature) than previous studies, which could have important implications on materials processing protocols and requirements for device sealing to minimize exposure to moisture. This result could explain why some of the most stable reported solar cells use procedures that eliminate moisture exposure by depositing in situ barrier layers41 or encapsulating in inert environments.42
## Outlook and Conclusion
The protocols established in this study introduce a specimen preparative method for sensitive materials systems like hybrid PSCs under experimentally defined environmental conditions including UV illumination and moisture exposure. By engineering the freezing process to preserve the authentic nanostructures at nanoscale and closely monitoring the cumulative electron exposure to minimize its radiation damage effects, we successfully retrieve the structure signatures of ${\mathsf{M A P b}}{\mathsf{b}}{\mathsf{l}}_{3}$ solar cell materials at atomic-resolution level. Single crystal $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ NWs were used as a model system to develop a fundamental understanding of degradation without the confounding effects of grain boundaries and to maximize interface area. The next step characterizing perovskite devices with cryo-EM will leverage a process for cross-sectioning full perovskite devices using a cryo-focused-ion beam (cryo-FIB) process that has been widely applied in the biology field. This will enable the observation of device-specific degradation modes that result from operational conditions, such as interface chemistry, halide segregation in solar cells, and halide exchange in LEDs. The developed protocol will open up future studies of more complex perovskite materials (i.e., 2D Ruddlesden-Popper perovskites) for optoelectronic applications and other electron-beam-sensitive materials with unprecedented understanding of their atomic-scale structures and chemistries.
## EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURES
## Synthesis of Hybrid Perovskite Nanostructures
First, lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b}|_{2})$ was spin-coated onto a glass slide $11\ \mathsf{c m}\times\mathsf{1.5\ c m})$ at 3,000 rpm for 30 s from a solution of $400\mathrm{mg/mLPbl}_{2}$ $99.99\%$ TCI) in dimethylformamide (DMF). Then, inside a nitrogen glovebox, the ${\mathsf{P b}}|_{2}$ -coated glass slide was immersed into 2 mL $C H_{3}N H_{3}|$ (MAI), $C H_{3}N H_{3}B r$ (MABr), or $\mathsf{M A l}_{0.5}\mathsf{B r}_{0.5}$ precursor solution, with $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ side facing up, to synthesize $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ , $\mathsf{M A P b}\mathsf{B r}_{3},$ and $\mathsf{M A P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.5}\mathsf{B r}_{0.5})_{3}\uparrow$ NWs, respectively. The precursor solution was prepared by dissolving MAI, MABr, or $\mathsf{M A l}_{0.5}\mathsf{B r}_{0.5}$ isopropanol (IPA) with a concentration of $20~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ . Before spin coating, the substrates were sequentially cleaned in ultrasonic baths of Extran 300 detergent (diluted 1:10 in deionized [DI] water), DI water, acetone, and IPA followed by 15 min of UV-ozone treatment (Jelight UVO-Cleaner Model 42). $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ -coated glass was heated at $\mathsf{100^{\circ}C}$ inside a nitrogen glovebox to remove the remnant solvent and water before being placed into the precursor solution. After $12\mathsf{h},$ the glass substrate was removed and rinsed with $1\mathsf{m L}$ anhydrous isopropanol (Sigma-Aldrich) to remove any leftover salt on the surface, dried under a stream of nitrogen flow, and then dried on a hot plate at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for another $30\mathsf{s}$ .
## X-Ray Diffraction
As-synthesized or UV-illuminated perovskite nanowires on glass were sealed with Kapton tape to avoid degradation from moisture during $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray diffraction measurement. Then, q–2q measurements were performed using a Panalytical X’Pert Pro Diffractometer (copper anode, $\mathsf{K}\alpha1=1.54060\mathring{\mathsf{A}},\mathsf{K}\alpha2=1.54443\mathring{\mathsf{A}},\mathsf{K}\alpha2/\mathsf{K}\alpha1$ ratio $=0.50\mathrm{;}$ .
